+### Radial Colormap Visualization
+A direct multivariate volume rendering technique where voxels are mapped to a radial colormap based on the values of their multiple channels, allowing visualization of cross-channel interactions and phase/composite identification [S1]. Map each voxel to the radial colormap according to the values of its channels: a voxel high in channel A appears close to the node for A; a voxel high in channel B appears close to the node for B; a voxel containing a mixture of channels appears in between the respective nodes [S1]. Plot a subsampled distribution of voxels within the display to provide a general idea of the voxel distribution relative to the radial colormap [S1]. The radial colormap has distinct nodes corresponding to each channel (e.g., Ce, Gd) in the multivariate dataset [S1]. Used in the RadVolViz research application and the MultivariateView prototype for visualizing multivariate volume datasets such as the MIEC dataset and medical datasets (knee sarcoma) [S1].
+
+### Multivalued Volume Error Metric
+A single error metric for multivalued volume data (multiple scalar and/or vector fields) used for view-dependent visualization decisions [S2]. Defined as $E_m(T) = \sum_{i=1}^{k} b_i E_{s,i}(T) + \sum_{i=1}^{l} b_{k+i} E_{v,i}(T)$ where $k$ is the number of scalar fields, $l$ is the number of vector fields, $E_{s,i}$ is the error for the i-th scalar field, $E_{v,i}$ is the error for the i-th vector field, and $b_i$ are normalization coefficients [S2]. The coefficients are $b_i = \frac{1}{v_{\delta,i}^2 \beta}$ where $v_{\delta,i} = |v_{max,i} - v_{min,i}|$ is the range of the i-th error component, and $\beta$ is the power used in the function $g$ [S2]. Coefficients $b_i$ act as normalization coefficients to make the influence of all scalar and vector fields equal [S2].
+
+### Candy Cane Visualization
+A two-field visualization technique where enstrophy defines the opacity and the Seed LIC texture of the vorticity field defines the color, creating vortex tube structures resembling 'candy canes' [S3]. Calculate the enstrophy field ($\epsilon = |\omega|^2$) from the vorticity field [S3]. Generate a Seed LIC texture for the vorticity field to convey directional structure [S3]. Map enstrophy values to opacity using a transfer function [S3]. Map the Seed LIC texture values to color [S3]. Render the volume using direct volume rendering with the defined transfer functions [S3].
+
+### Multi-variate Volume Rendering
+A rendering approach that handles multiple data fields (e.g., temperature and density) simultaneously, often using multi-variate transfer functions [S4, unofficial].
+
+### Vector Field Polar Coordinate Mapping
+A representation of vector fields in polar coordinates (magnitude, azimuth, elevation) to map vector properties to visual parameters (opacity, hue, saturation) [S5, unofficial]. Designate the free stream velocity direction as the zero direction vector (axis) [S5, unofficial]. Calculate the azimuth (rotational) angle around the free stream vector [S5, unofficial]. Map the azimuth angle to Hue. The alignment is arbitrary; the y-axis direction was used as zero in the implementation [S5, unofficial]. Calculate the elevation (upstream to downstream) angle the field vector makes with the free stream vector [S5, unofficial]. Map the elevation angle to Saturation. Vectors parallel to free stream are nearly gray (unsaturated); vectors 180 degrees to free stream are maximally saturated [S5, unofficial]. Map the magnitude of the vector to Opacity [S5, unofficial]. The free stream velocity direction is selected as the axis for zero direction [S5, unofficial].
+
+### Velocity Component Visualization
+Visualizing specific components of the velocity field (e.g., x-component) to identify flow separation and reversal [S5, unofficial]. Map the smallest x velocity values (including negative values) to cyan and high opacity [S5, unofficial]. Map the largest x velocity components to yellow and near transparency [S5, unofficial].
+
+### Continuous Indexed Points for Multivariate Volume Visualization
+A technique to improve multivariate volume visualization by representing linear structures in parallel coordinates to encode local correlation of multivariate volume data using continuous indexed points [S6]. Perform local linear fitting in the spatial neighborhood of each volume sample using principal component analysis, accelerated by hierarchical spatial data structures [S6]. Visualize the local linear information as continuous indexed points in parallel coordinates, which is a density representation of indexed points in a continuous domain [S6]. Utilize both 1-flat and 2-flat indexed points to identify correlations between two variables and three variables, respectively [S6]. Apply an interactive occlusion shading model to facilitate good spatial perception of the volume rendering of volumetric correlation characteristics [S6]. Support interactive exploration using specifically designed multivariate transfer function widgets working in the image plane of parallel coordinates [S6].
+
+### Shape Space Scatterplot
+A 2D plot depicting the relation between two scalar invariants, specifically principal stresses, to quantify normal and shear stresses [S7, book]. Sort the three principal stresses in descending order: σ1 ≥ σ2 ≥ σ3 [S7, book]. Plot σ1 on the x-axis and σ3 on the y-axis, dividing the plot into four quadrants (A, B, C, D) [S7, book]. Apply a sign-preserving logarithmic mapping to the eigenvalues: f(σi) = log(σi + 1) for σi ≥ 0, and f(σi) = −log(1 − σi) for σi < 0 [S7, book]. Linearly map the results of the logarithmic mapping to the range [-1, 1] [S7, book]. Unlike standard logarithmic mappings that require positive domains, this method preserves signs to distinguish between positive (tensile) and negative (compressive) invariants [S7, book].
 
 ## Background
-The visualization of vector and tensor fields is a fundamental problem in scientific visualization, arising in fluid dynamics, electromagnetism, and medical imaging (specifically Diffusion Tensor Imaging). Early methods relied on glyph-based approaches (arrows, lines) for their intuitive geometric representation. As computational power increased, GPU-based implementations allowed for dense glyph rendering. Color-based methods, such as domain coloring for complex fields, provide a dense alternative to glyphs. The integration of these techniques into volume rendering pipelines allows for the simultaneous visualization of scalar context (e.g., density) and vector/tensor structure.
+The visualization of vector and tensor fields is a fundamental problem in scientific visualization, arising in fluid dynamics, electromagnetism, and medical imaging (specifically Diffusion Tensor Imaging) [G]. Early methods relied on glyph-based approaches (arrows, lines) for their intuitive geometric representation [G]. As computational power increased, GPU-based implementations allowed for dense glyph rendering [G]. Color-based methods, such as domain coloring for complex fields, provide a dense alternative to glyphs [G]. The integration of these techniques into volume rendering pipelines allows for the simultaneous visualization of scalar context and vector/tensor structure [G].
 
